{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-85977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate of growth of the bear population $P(t)$ in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(4\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Find an expression for the bear population after $t$ years, given that there were $150$ bears initially." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=4\\,500.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-(4\\,500\\cdot 0.2)t}}=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-900t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=150$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-900\\cdot 0}} & =150 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{4\\,500}{150} \\\\ C & =30-1 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-900t}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-85977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "P(t)=\\frac{3\\,000}{1-29{e}^{-900t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+150{e}^{-900t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-900t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-90t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1-29{e}^{-900t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-900t}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-119861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate of growth in the number of fishes $P(t)$ with a fungal infection in a certain pond is jointly proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t$ and the number that have not been infected. If the constant of proportionality is $0.009$ and the carrying capacity is $130,$ then find an expression for the number of fishes with fungal infection, given that there were $5$ fishes infected initially." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-119861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the growth rate of infected fishes is *jointly* proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t,$ and the number that have not, the number of infected fishes follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.009,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=130.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of fishes with fungal infection is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.009P(130-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-(130\\cdot 0.009)t}}=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17\\cdot 0}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{130}{5} \\\\ C & =26-1 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-119861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-119861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-119861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-0.009t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{25}{1+{e}^{-0.009t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{25}{1+{e}^{-t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{5}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-119861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-353253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the number of residents using a new fitness app, $P(t),$ in a small town is jointly proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t,$ measured in months, and the difference between the town’s maximum potential users and the number of residents currently using the app.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum potential number of users is $800,$ find an expression for the number of residents using the app after $t$ months, given that there were $100$ users initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $800$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-353253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the number of residents using the app grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum potential number of users and the number currently using the app, the number of residents using the app follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.005\\,P(800-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-(800\\cdot 0.005)t}}=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{800}{100} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-353253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-353253" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-353253" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7e^{-4t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the mouse population $P(t)$ in a certain field is jointly proportional to the mouse population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the field's carrying capacity and the mouse population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,062\\,5$ and the carrying capacity is $8\\,000,$ then find an expression for the mouse population after $t$ years, given that there were $1\\,000$ mice in the field initially." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the mouse population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the mouse population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the mouse population, the mouse population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,062\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8\\,000.$ Therefore, the mouse population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,062\\,5P(8\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(8\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,062\\,5)t}}=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 0}} & =1\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{8\\,000}{1\\,000} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-83875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-2t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.2t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.5t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the crab population $P(t)$ in a certain river is jointly proportional to the crab population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the river's carrying capacity and the crab population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,02$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000,$ then find an expression for the crab population after $t$ years, given that there were $1\\,000$ crab in the river initially." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the crab population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the crab population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the crab population, the crab population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,02,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore, the crab population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,02P(10\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,02t)}}=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2\\cdot 0}} & =1\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{10\\,000}{1\\,000} \\\\ C & =10-1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+9{e}^{-0.2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-83868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-2t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.2t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-2t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+9{e}^{-0.2t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+9{e}^{-0.2t}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $5000.$ How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the squirrel population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the squirrel population, the squirrel population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5000.$ Therefore, the squirrel population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,01P(5000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-(5000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=4000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =4000 \\\\ \\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05(0)}} & =4000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ C & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=10$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & ≈4342.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-83771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4342"] ["math" "4354"] ["math" "4582"] ["math" "4872"] ["math" "4972"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4342"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-347875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of rabbits on a particular island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P-0.0002{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. If there were $400$ rabbits on the island initially, how many rabbits inhabited the island after $3$ years? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $3$ years, approximately\n{{field-1}} rabbits inhabited the island." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-347875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.2P-0.0002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0002P(1000-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-(1000\\cdot 0.0002)t}}=\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=400$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2(0)}} & =400 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1000}{400} \\\\ C & =2.5-1 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1000}{1+1.5{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =\\frac{1000}{1+1.5{e}^{-0.2(3)}} \\\\  & ≈548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ years, approximately $548$ rabbits inhabited the island." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-347875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-347875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-347875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "548"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the deer population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the deer population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the deer population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,03$ and the carrying capacity is $9000.$ How many deers will there be in the forest after $8$ years given that there were $1800$ deers initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the deer population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the deer population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the deer population, the deer population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,03,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=9000.$ Therefore, the deer population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,03P(9000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-(9000\\cdot 0.000\\,03)t}}=\\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-0.27t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1800$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1800 \\\\ \\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-0.27(0)}} & =1800 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{9000}{1800} \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{9000}{1+4{e}^{-0.27\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=8$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & =\\frac{9000}{1+4{e}^{-0.27\\cdot 8}} \\\\  & ≈6159.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-85978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6159"] ["math" "6897"] ["math" "7787"] ["math" "7798"] ["math" "8791"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-85978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6159"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-120253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate in the number $P(t)$ of files infected with a virus in a computer is jointly proportional to the number of infected files at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the difference between the computer's carrying capacity and the number of infected files. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,005$ and the carrying capacity is $100\\,000.$ How many files will have the virus after $12$ hours given that there were $1000$ files with the virus initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-120253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the number of files infected with the virus' growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the number of infected files at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of infected files, the number of files infected with the virus follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100\\,000.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of files infected with the virus is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,005P(100\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,005)t}}=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1000 \\\\ \\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5(0)}} & =1000 \\\\ 1+C & =100 \\\\ C & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=12$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 12}} \\\\  & ≈80\\,296.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-120253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-120253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-120253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "100\\,000"] ["math" "1\\,638"] ["math" "5\\,000"] ["math" "80\\,296"] ["math" "99"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-120253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "80\\,296"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of turtles on a particular island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.05P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. If there were $5$ turtles on the island initially, how many turtles inhabited the island after $11$ years? Round you answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.05P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,5P(100-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\cdot 0.000\\,5)t}}=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.05(0)}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =20 \\\\ C & =19\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=11$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(11) & =\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.05(11)}} \\\\  & ≈8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $11$ years, approximately $8$ turtles inhabited the island." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67203974bf1c9c30a2d91854dd4428f13a604e0a9ea9b71b04eadeaead1257e4"} {"question" "q-83895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "19"] ["math" "27"] ["math" "33"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "q-83895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/state.json" "file_sha256" "3862b508fbc186700bd7d8a89dc07f9a4a3db7e4b78729020bd0e376e022c7a4"} {"question" "e-7408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate of growth of the panda population $P(t)$ in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.001\\,25 P(800-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observations. Find an expression for the panda population after $t$ years, given that there were $400$ pandas initially." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/example-7408.json" "file_sha256" "34249f0f529c94edba64659d093b2dc1c48ccbdd5b8177104682ec02028a6454"} {"question" "e-7408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.001\\,25,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) \n= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-(800\\cdot 0.001\\,25)t}}= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=400$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0}} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{800}{1+C} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{1+C}{800} & =\\frac{1}{400} \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{800}{400} \\\\ C & =2-1 \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the panda population is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)= \\dfrac{800}{1+e^{-t}} .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/example-7408.json" "file_sha256" "34249f0f529c94edba64659d093b2dc1c48ccbdd5b8177104682ec02028a6454"} {"question" "e-7407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the hare population $P(t)$ in a particular forest is jointly proportional to the hare population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hare population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000.$ How many hares will there be in the forest after $7$ years given that there were $2\\,500$ hares initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/example-7407.json" "file_sha256" "7a2b90b32c692bc6bc5765e594d800dd1d510a2d9ae59c61533ab2fc0bd9b34b"} {"question" "e-7407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the hare population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hare population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hare population, the hare population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore, the hare population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.000\\,01 P(10\\,000-P) .\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) \n= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-0.1t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2\\,500$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.1(0)}} & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{10\\,000}{1+C} & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{1+C}{10\\,000} & =\\frac{1}{2\\,500} \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{10\\,000}{2\\,500} \\\\ 1+C & =4 \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\frac{10\\,000}{1+3 e^{-0.1 t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of hares in the forest after $7$ years, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{10\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 7}} \\\\  & ≈4\\,016.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048720/example-7407.json" "file_sha256" "7a2b90b32c692bc6bc5765e594d800dd1d510a2d9ae59c61533ab2fc0bd9b34b"}] "usage" {"q-83771" [] "e-7408" [] "e-7407" []} "basis" 1710 "retractions" [[17592186180224 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.00001$ and the carrying capacity is $5000$. How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer."] [17592186179463 :question/problem "The rate of growth of the panda population $P(t)$ in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model is\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.001\\,25 P(800-P)$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observations. Find an expression for the panda population after $t$ years, given that there were $400$ pandas initially."] [17592186179463 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is\n\n$$\nk=0.001\\,25\n$$\n\nand the carrying capacity is $a=800$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-(800\\cdot 0.001\\,25)t}}= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-t}}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition\n\n$$\nP(0)=400\n$$\n\nand solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{800}{1 + Ce^{-0}} &= 400 \\\\\n\\frac{800}{1 + C} &= 400 \\\\\n\\frac{1 + C}{800} &= \\frac{1}{400} \\\\\n1 + C &= \\frac{800}{400} \\\\\nC &= 2 - 1 \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the panda population is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)= \\dfrac{800}{1+e^{-t}}\n$$"] [17592186179462 :question/problem "The growth rate of the hare population $P(t)$ in a particular forest is jointly proportional to the hare population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hare population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000$. How many hares will there be in the forest after $7$ years given that there were $2\\,500$ hares initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer."] [17592186179462 :question/worked-solution "Since the hare population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hare population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hare population, the hare population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality\n\n$$\nk=0.000\\,01\n$$\n\nand the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000$. Therefore, the hare population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.000\\,01 P(10\\,000-P)\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-0.1t}}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition\n\n$$\nP(0)=2\\,500\n$$\n\nand solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0) &= 2500 \\\\\n(10000)/(1 + Ce^{-0.1(0)}) &= 2500 \\\\\n\\frac{10000}{1 + C} &= 2500 \\\\\n\\frac{1 + C}{10000} &= \\frac{1}{2500} \\\\\n1 + C &= \\frac{10000}{2500} \\\\\n1 + C &= 4 \\\\\nC &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\frac{10\\,000}{1+3 e^{-0.1 t}}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of hares in the forest after $7$ years, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t)$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(7) &= \\frac{10000}{1 + 3e^{-0.1 \\cdot 7}} \\\\\n&\\approx 4016\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186179220 17592186179221 17592186179222 17592186179223 17592186179224] "immutable_fields" [[17592186179219 :answer-field/correct] [17592186179219 :answer-field/id] [17592186179219 :answer-field/key] [17592186179219 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
